4. STEP-BY-STEP INSTRUCTIONS TO RUN THE SCRIPT

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4.1. Step 1: Create the Project Directory

1. Open Windows File Explorer.

2. Navigate to a directory of your choice, such as C:\PrinterUtility or your 

   Desktop.

3. Create a new folder named PrinterTool.



4.2. Step 2: Save the Python File

1. Open a text editor such as Notepad or VS Code.

2. Copy and paste the complete Python code provided in Section 3 into the 

   editor.

3. Select File > Save As.

4. In the "Save as type" dropdown, select All Files (*.*).

5. Save the file as printer_tool.py inside the PrinterTool folder.

   (Note: When saving in Notepad, make sure Encoding is set to UTF-8 so the 

   emoji icons render properly.)



4.3. Step 3: Run the Application

1. Double-click printer_tool.py or launch it from the Command Prompt:



   python printer_tool.py



2. User Account Control (UAC) Prompt: Because the script detects elevation 

   requirements upon launch, Windows will display a prompt asking: "Do you want 

   to allow this app to make changes to your device?" Click Yes.

3. The graphical interface will appear.

4. Click RUN DIAGNOSTICS & CLEAN to execute the maintenance routine. Real-time 

   progress and logs will appear in the output console.





5. COMPILING INTO A STANDALONE EXECUTABLE (.EXE)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

You can package printer_tool.py into a single, standalone .exe binary that runs 

on any Windows machine without requiring Python to be installed.



5.1. Step 1: Install PyInstaller

Open Command Prompt or Windows PowerShell as Administrator and run:



   pip install pyinstaller



5.2. Step 2: Compile the Executable

Navigate to the directory where printer_tool.py is saved and run the build 

command:



   cd /d C:\PrinterTool

   pyinstaller --onefile --noconsole --name "PrinterTroubleshooter" printer_tool.py



5.3. Parameter Breakdown

* --onefile: Bundles the entire Python runtime, dependencies, and script into 

  a single, portable executable file.

* --noconsole: Suppresses the default background black command prompt window, 

  ensuring only the modern GUI is visible.

* --name "PrinterTroubleshooter": Assigns a user-friendly name to the output 

  executable.



5.4. Step 3: Locate and Run Your .exe

1. Once PyInstaller completes compilation, a dist folder will appear in your 

   directory (C:\PrinterTool\dist).

2. Inside dist, you will find PrinterTroubleshooter.exe.

3. Double-click PrinterTroubleshooter.exe to run the tool directly. You can 

   distribute this single file to any Windows computer on your network.





6. ARCHITECTURE & IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[GUI Framework]

- Implementation: tkinter & tkinter.scrolledtext

- Purpose: Provides lightweight, native, zero-dependency graphical windows and 

  log views.



[Auto-Elevation]

- Implementation: ctypes.windll.shell32.IsUserAnAdmin() & ShellExecuteW("runas")

- Purpose: Detects administrator status and re-launches the process with 

  elevated security tokens when needed.



[Thread Management]

- Implementation: threading.Thread(target=...)

- Purpose: Offloads system calls to a background worker thread, ensuring the 

  GUI never freezes or enters "Not Responding" states.



[System Interaction]

- Implementation: subprocess.Popen with CREATE_NO_WINDOW

- Purpose: Spawns PowerShell quietly in the background and pipes stdout 

  line-by-line into the UI console in real time.



[Service Control]

- Implementation: Stop-Service & Start-Service

- Purpose: Directly interfaces with Windows Service Manager to cleanly toggle 

  the Spooler service.



[Spool Purge]

- Implementation: Remove-Item on *.spl and *.shd

- Purpose: Purges corrupt, orphaned print spool files from disk.





7. TROUBLESHOOTING & FAQ

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q1: The ping test shows "OFFLINE" even though my printer is turned on.

Cause: Network printers configured with ICMP ping disabled or machines 

connected across certain enterprise subnets/VPNs block ping packets.

Resolution: The script will still proceed to clear stuck local print queues 

and cycle the spooler service regardless of ping status.



Q2: Windows Defender or Antivirus flags the compiled .exe.

Cause: Freshly compiled executables created by PyInstaller lack digital 

code-signing certificates and can trigger generic heuristic warnings.

Resolution: Add a security exception in Windows Defender for the file, or run 

the native Python script (python printer_tool.py) directly.



Q3: Some print jobs cannot be deleted during Step 4.

Cause: A hardware driver or third-party print monitor process holds a locked 

handle on the spool file.

Resolution: Reboot the machine to drop all file locks, then immediately 

execute the troubleshooter before submitting new print jobs.

